# Line endings are not a matter of taste in this repository.
#
# Git on Windows is usually installed with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites
# CRLF to LF on the way into a commit and back again on checkout. For most
# files that is invisible and harmless. For the batch files it is neither:
# cmd.exe reads a .bat by byte offset and seeks back and forth through it to
# find labels, and that bookkeeping assumes CRLF. With LF endings it mostly
# works - the worst possible failure, because it keeps working until a label
# moves, and then one GOTO lands somewhere that is not a label and the run
# stops with "The system cannot find the batch label specified".
#
# tests/bomcheck.sh catches that in a working tree. This file is what stops it
# being introduced in the first place, by whoever next commits from a machine
# whose git is set up to convert.

# Everything normalises to LF in the repository, whatever the contributor's
# own setting, so that nobody's autocrlf decides the matter.
* text=auto

# Except these. -text means "never convert, in either direction": the blobs
# are stored with CRLF already and every checkout, export and source download
# gets them back byte for byte. Normalising to LF and converting on checkout
# would also work for a clone, but not for someone who downloads the source
# as a zip from GitHub and runs the tools out of it.
*.bat   -text
*.cmd   -text

# And the reverse case: a shell script with CRLF endings fails on Linux with
# a bad-interpreter error, because the carriage return is read as part of the
# command name.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Binaries, so git never inspects or rewrites them.
*.msi   binary
*.ico   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.mp4   binary
